\f1\b0 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 Official Review\cf0 \cb1 \strokec2 by Reviewer Z18218 Jun 2026, 13:25 (modified: 28 Jul 2026, 19:43)Program Chairs, Senior Area Chairs, Area Chairs, Reviewers Submitted, Authors, Ethics Reviewers, Ethics Chairs, Reviewer Z182{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openreview.net/revisions?id=5B6becfDbs"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 Revisions}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Summary:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 This paper explores the use of Small Language Models (here, fine-tuned versions of GPT-2) as analysis tools to help researchers in reflexive study of texts, in this case, analysing political polarization bias around gender across a pair of datasets. Gender-related articles were collected from two US political news websites that are respectively politically left (ACLU) and politically right (The Heritage Foundation) oriented. A copy of the GPT-2 SLM was fine-tuned on each dataset respectively. Four short gender prompts (e.g. "Femininity is...") are then used to get a corpus of generations from each model. The generations are analysed through close reading to identify 'synthetic middle' ground between the political viewpoints.\
The paper is very unlike a 'traditional' NeurIPS paper (whatever that is), which makes it a little hard to apply the reviewing guidelines ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://neurips.cc/Conferences/2026/ReviewerGuidelines"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 \strokec5 https://neurips.cc/Conferences/2026/ReviewerGuidelines}}). To this end, I have reviewed it in good faith from a perspective that treats it as a kind of methods paper.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Contribution Type:
\f1\b0  General: Most submissions will fall into this type.\

\f0\b Strengths And Weaknesses:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Strengths\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A novel approach to studying polarization and political bias specifically, and a novel use of SLMs to support reflexive research generally\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Contrast dataset construction is well motivated\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Writing is clear, and the finding around reality and subjectivity in this specific case study is interesting.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Weaknesses\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 To the extent that the paper is interested in studying the US political left and right, the use of ACLU and The Heritage Foundation as proxies for these concepts or groups needs further discussion. E.g. discussion on the stated values of these organizations, their platform audiences and leadership or staff makeup, analysis of the contents of the respective datasets, are all important contextual details that underlie the findings and method being demonstrated in the paper. In some sense these details bound the scope of the findings, but discussion of them is missing from the paper. E.g. keyword histogram analysis of the contents of each dataset might be one starting point for contrasting them.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 To the extent that the paper advocates for a new methodology (using SLMs in this way), it hinges on researchers knowing how to do close reading of the generated completions. In this reviewer's opinion, the NeurIPS audience is unlikely to have much overlap with this category of researcher, potentially limiting it's relevance to this community.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The results (model generations highlighted in the article) are charitably qualitative, or uncharitably, appear cherry-picked. I understand this kind of research method is non-quantitative, however more detail to aid reproducibility is necessary - e.g. including the full set of generations from each model in an appendix.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The fine-tuning process needs to be described in more detail to aid reproducibility. Which optimizer, software library, hyper-parameters were used.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Generation / decoding parameters for model prompting are not mentioned.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Quality:
\f1\b0  2: not good\

\f0\b Clarity:
\f1\b0  3: good\

\f0\b Significance:
\f1\b0  2: not good\

\f0\b Originality:
\f1\b0  3: good\

\f0\b Questions:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In what ways could the method you outline be made more quantitative, numerical, or automated - in the interest of helping aid it's reception with computationally-minded researchers? Or, does this method fundamentally resist quantification?\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Can you give examples of other tasks (beyond synthetic middle identification in political polarization) that SLMs might be useful for in a similar fashion?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Limitations:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A single SLM model is studied, and a single issue is studied (political polarization around gender) - making it unclear how generalizable the method and approach is beyond this case study. Trying the same approach with several SLMs would aid other researchers hoping to try the same technique.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The four prompts used for generation are not motivated. Why these four? Clarifying the selection or identification of these would help the reader.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

